{"name":"Ancient Earth","hex":"#746550","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ancient","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having lasted from a remote period; having been of long duration; of great age, very old.","Existent or occurring in time long past, usually in remote ages; belonging to or associated with antiquity; old, as opposed to modern."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who is very old.","A person who lived in ancient times."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ancient"},{"term":"earth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Earth (“the third planet of the Solar System, upon which humans live”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soil.","Any general rock-based material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ancient history","blocks":[{"text":"Ancient history is a time period from the beginning of writing and recorded human history through late antiquity. The span of recorded history is roughly 5,000 years, beginning with the development of Sumerian cuneiform script. Ancient history covers all continents inhabited by humans in the period 3000 BC – AD 500."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum%2C_London_%288747049029%29_%282%29.jpg/330px-Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum%2C_London_%288747049029%29_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_history","wikidata":"Q41493"},{"title":"Earth","blocks":[{"text":"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only astronomical object known to harbor life. This is made possible by Earth being an ocean world, the only one in the Solar System sustaining liquid surface water. Almost all of Earth's water is contained in its ocean, which covers 70.8% of Earth's crust. The remaining 29.2% of Earth's crust is dry land, which is predominantly located within Earth's land hemisphere in the form of continental landmasses. Most of Earth's land is at least somewhat humid and covered by vegetation, while large ice sheets at Earth's polar deserts retain more water than Earth's groundwater, lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water combined. Earth's crust consists of slowly moving tectonic plates, which interact to produce mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes. Earth has a liquid outer core that generates a magnetosphere capable of deflecting most of the destructive solar winds and cosmic radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg/330px-Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth","wikidata":"Q2"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg/500px-CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg/1280px-CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg/500px-CairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACairoEgMuseumTaaMaskMostlyPhotographed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Mohenjo-daro_Priesterk%C3%B6nig.jpeg/500px-Mohenjo-daro_Priesterk%C3%B6nig.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Mohenjo-daro_Priesterk%C3%B6nig.jpeg/500px-Mohenjo-daro_Priesterk%C